<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
  <title>[Feature title] — Briger Hall Discovery Guide</title>
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="style.css">
  <link rel="preconnect" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com">
  <link href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Fraunces:wght@600&amp;family=Source+Sans+3&amp;family=IBM+Plex+Mono&amp;display=swap" rel="stylesheet">
</head>
<body>
  <a class="skip-link" href="#main-content">Skip to feature guide</a>
  <main class="station" id="main-content">
    <header>
      <div class="eyebrow">Briger Hall Discovery Guide</div>
      <!-- Replace the title here AND in the title tag above. -->
      <h1>[Feature title]</h1>
      <p class="intro">[Introduce your feature and the environmental question you will explore. Write for a curious visitor with no background in the topic.]</p>
    </header>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="find-heading">
      <h2 id="find-heading"><span class="marker" aria-hidden="true">1</span> Find it</h2>
      <p><strong>Where to find it:</strong> [Floor, nearby landmark, and clear directions within Briger Hall.]</p>
      <!-- REQUIRED: Upload an original photo or sketch to images/. Avoid photos of people.
           Replace the filename, alt text, and caption. Paths are case-sensitive. -->
      <figure class="feature-image">
        <img src="images/your-feature.jpg" alt="[Describe your photo or sketch, including the relevant visible detail.]">
        <figcaption>[Describe the image and credit the photographer or sketch artist.]</figcaption>
      </figure>
      <div class="activity">
        <p><strong>Look closely:</strong> [Invite the visitor to notice one specific detail of the actual feature.]</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="interesting-heading">
      <h2 id="interesting-heading"><span class="marker" aria-hidden="true">2</span> Why is it interesting?</h2>
      <p><strong>Our observation:</strong> [Describe at least one thing you observed yourself. A useful count or measurement is optional.]</p>
      <p>[Explain what you learned about the feature or broader topic. Cite checked background claims here.] <a href="#source-1">[1]</a></p>
      <!-- Distinguish what you observed from what a source explains. Do not assume a
           general source verifies a technical specification of this building. -->
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="what-if-heading">
      <h2 id="what-if-heading"><span class="marker" aria-hidden="true">3</span> What if / What might change?</h2>
      <div class="counterfactual">
        <p class="scenario-label">Hypothetical alternative</p>
        <p>[State one plausible alternative to this feature or collection experience. Compare options serving a similar purpose, or explain what would be lost if the feature were absent.]</p>
      </div>
      <p><strong>For the environment:</strong> [Explain at least one environmental consequence and HOW it would arise. Support the mechanism with a relevant source.] <a href="#source-2">[2]</a></p>
      <p><strong>For people:</strong> [Explain at least one human benefit, burden, or tradeoff, and how it would arise. Cite sources where relevant.]</p>
      <p class="uncertainty"><strong>What we do not know:</strong> [Identify an honest uncertainty or assumption. Explain what condition or missing information could change the comparison.]</p>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="sources-heading">
      <h2 id="sources-heading"><span class="marker" aria-hidden="true">4</span> Show how you know</h2>
      <!-- REQUIRED: At least two relevant sources beyond AI output. Replace these
           example URLs and descriptions. Add entries and inline citations as needed. -->
      <ol class="sources">
        <li id="source-1">[Author or organization]. <a href="https://example.com/source-1">[Source title]</a>. [Date, if available.] [What information does this source support?]</li>
        <li id="source-2">[Author or organization]. <a href="https://example.com/source-2">[Source title]</a>. [Date, if available.] [What information does this source support?]</li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section class="section" aria-labelledby="listen-heading">
      <h2 id="listen-heading"><span class="marker" aria-hidden="true">5</span> Listen and look</h2>
      <!-- REQUIRED: Create an AI-narrated guide or conversational podcast clip with
           ElevenLabs, UNDER 4 minutes. Listen, correct, and regenerate as needed.
           Include: a detail to examine, checked explanation, your counterfactual,
           environmental and human tradeoffs, honest uncertainty, and a visitor question.
           Upload your MP3 to audio/ and replace BOTH paths below. -->
      <figure class="audio-guide">
        <figcaption id="audio-caption">[Audio title] — AI-generated [narrated guide or conversational podcast], [duration]. [Briefly describe what visitors will hear.]</figcaption>
        <audio controls preload="metadata" src="audio/your-feature.mp3" aria-describedby="audio-caption">
          Your browser does not support the audio player. Use the MP3 link below.
        </audio>
        <p><a href="audio/your-feature.mp3">Open the MP3 audio</a></p>
      </figure>
      <details class="transcript">
        <summary>Read the complete audio transcript</summary>
        <!-- Replace with the COMPLETE transcript of the final recording, not a summary.
             Use paragraphs; label speakers if conversational. Update if audio changes. -->
        <div class="transcript-text">
          <p>[Paste the complete transcript here, including the invitation to look, explanation, counterfactual, environmental and human tradeoffs, uncertainty, and visitor question.]</p>
        </div>
      </details>
      <div class="activity">
        <p><strong>Consider:</strong> [Repeat the visitor question from your recording so someone reading without audio can consider it too.]</p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- BEFORE SUBMITTING: Replace all bracketed text, example URLs, and media paths.
         Check the published page, image, audio, transcript, and links on a phone or
         in a signed-out browser. Test with 3 people outside class, keep anonymous
         notes, and make at least one revision based on feedback.
         Submit your page link, revisions, and reflection separately to the instructor. -->
  </main>
</body>
</html>
